#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111863 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have an angle ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) in the coordinate plane positioned in the usual way. Its **reference angle**, denoted ![](images/89/89ec1e1e3e3e50f78767b2b22daab75389f0186bdab3b72652b22ef5ddd2c956.svg) is the smallest positive acute angle formed with the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis. \n\nFor example, let's consider the angles ![](images/1d/1d2f5a08ccb5688494cc87753b29b5569af4bbd93c704c376f30046928f8618e.svg) and ![](images/5b/5b7ef934f94884d2eaceec6dffd5ff9fd12db70a221b3c3b3bce23b6e48efcc1.svg) These angles lie in the first, second, third, and fourth quadrants, respectively. And each of these angles has a reference angle ![](images/27/27ca7b17696552bcbf9d92c69a6ea8dac7e0e56f0f450e12dd1ad3e0a98abc6c.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfba74ff2c6a69b3db1c9478f5caac4c31578ed93b47302a74d843b04fc6ee85.png)\n\n**Note:** Any reference angle always lies between ![](images/d2/d22511f2212b05e9408f52f8da19a125897737f033bb64f480780f2f024b2091.svg) and ![](images/26/2614c435bff188ded3027f2a8f1ea011b37b41a1d62d2ff4bae181ce9107b5c2.svg) inclusively:\n\n![](images/ac/acc49c928eb9101bcc2ce3c43238a8ed33742d0eb791fca361584d556bca8b9a.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2338474e-8937-5e5e-88ae-7e7848bf2d6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2492 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
